<!--{"title": "Migrating to stateless MCP: a 2026-07-28 playbook", "description": "The MCP 2026-07-28 spec removed sessions and the handshake. A playbook for migrating servers, gateways and clients: state handles, MRTR, headers, caching and auth changes.", "h1": "Migrating to stateless MCP: a playbook for the 2026-07-28 specification", "kicker": "AI architecture · MCP", "lede": "The 2026-07-28 Model Context Protocol specification turned a stateful, bidirectional protocol into a stateless request and response one. That is good news for anyone running MCP servers at scale, and a real migration for anyone who built on sessions, server-initiated requests or long-lived streams.", "date": "2026-10-28", "datetime": "2026-10-28T12:20:00+04:00", "readtime": "8 min read", "format": "playbook", "tags": ["ai", "mcp", "agents", "platform"], "keywords": ["MCP 2026-07-28", "stateless MCP", "MCP migration", "Multi Round-Trip Requests", "MRTR", "Mcp-Method header", "MCP gateway routing", "server/discover", "MCP deprecations", "AI architect"], "faq": [["What changed in MCP 2026-07-28?", "The initialize handshake and protocol-level sessions were removed, so every request carries its own protocol version and client capabilities. Server-initiated requests such as elicitation and sampling moved to Multi Round-Trip Requests, list results became cacheable, and Streamable HTTP requests must carry Mcp-Method and Mcp-Name headers."], ["Does stateless MCP mean my server cannot keep state?", "No. The specification removes state hidden in the transport. Servers that need state across calls mint an explicit handle from a tool and the model passes it back as an ordinary argument."], ["How long do deprecated MCP features keep working?", "The 2026-07-28 release introduced a formal deprecation policy with a twelve-month minimum window. Roots, Sampling and Logging were deprecated, and remain functional during that window."]], "related": ["/ai-architect/", "/fractional-cto/", "/technical-due-diligence/"], "citations": [{"title": "The 2026-07-28 Specification", "url": "https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol blog", "date": "28 Jul 2026"}, {"title": "Key changes (2026-07-28 changelog)", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol specification"}, {"title": "Multi Round-Trip Requests", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/patterns/mrtr", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol specification"}, {"title": "Streamable HTTP transport", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/streamable-http", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol specification"}, {"title": "Deprecated features", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/deprecated", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol specification"}]}-->
<p>Most early MCP servers were built like chat connections: a client connected, shook hands, held a session open and the server pushed requests back when it needed something. That worked on a laptop. It worked less well behind a load balancer, in serverless runtimes, or anywhere a connection could drop. The 2026-07-28 specification addresses that head on<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What changed, in one table</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Before</th><th>From 2026-07-28</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>initialize</code> handshake and <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code></td><td>Removed; each request carries protocol version and client capabilities in <code>_meta</code></td><td>Any request can land on any instance<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Capabilities learned at connect time</td><td>Optional <code>server/discover</code> call that servers must implement</td><td>Up-front version selection when clients want it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Server-initiated elicitation, sampling and roots requests</td><td>Multi Round-Trip Requests: the server returns <code>input_required</code> and the client retries with answers</td><td>No held-open bidirectional stream<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Method and tool names only in the JSON body</td><td><code>Mcp-Method</code> and <code>Mcp-Name</code> headers required on Streamable HTTP</td><td>Gateways route and authorise on headers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>List results with no freshness hints</td><td><code>ttlMs</code> and <code>cacheScope</code>, plus deterministic tool order</td><td>Client caching and stable prompt caches<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>SSE resumability with <code>Last-Event-ID</code></td><td>Removed; a broken stream means re-issuing the request</td><td>Simpler transport, but retries must be safe<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The release also moved Tasks into an official extension, hardened authorisation with RFC 9207 issuer validation, deprecated Dynamic Client Registration in favour of client metadata documents, and introduced a formal deprecation policy with a twelve-month minimum window<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The playbook</h2>
<h3>1. Inventory what depends on sessions</h3>
<p>List every server and find where it relies on connection state: per-session caches, "current project" set at connect time, server-pushed requests, resumable streams. Those are your migration items. Servers that only expose stateless tools usually need little more than an SDK upgrade.</p>

<h3>2. Replace hidden state with explicit handles</h3>
<p>The specification's answer to cross-call state is an explicit, server-minted handle returned by a tool and passed back as an ordinary argument<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. The maintainers note this works better than transport-hidden state because the model can see the handle and thread it between tools<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Store the state behind the handle in a shared store, scoped to the user, with an expiry.</p>

<h3>3. Rework interactive tools with MRTR</h3>
<p>Tools that asked the user for confirmation or a missing parameter mid-call now return an interim result with <code>resultType: "input_required"</code>; the client gathers the answers and retries the original request with them<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Design these tools so a retry is safe, and keep any correlation data in the request state rather than server memory.</p>

<h3>4. Make retries safe everywhere</h3>
<p>Without stream resumability, a dropped response stream means the client re-issues the request with a new ID<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Any tool with side effects needs idempotency, the same discipline as in <a href="/insights/idempotent-payments-order-pipelines/">idempotent payment pipelines</a>.</p>

<h3>5. Move routing and policy to the gateway</h3>
<p>With <code>Mcp-Method</code> and <code>Mcp-Name</code> on every Streamable HTTP request<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, a gateway can rate-limit, authorise and meter per tool without parsing bodies. Plain round-robin load balancing now works. This is the natural place to enforce the controls in <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">securing MCP servers</a>.</p>

<h3>6. Use the cache hints</h3>
<p>Return a deterministic tool order and sensible <code>ttlMs</code> values. Stable tool lists help clients cache catalogues and keep upstream prompt caches warm, which matters for cost, as covered in <a href="/insights/prompt-caching-llm-cost-latency/">prompt caching</a>.</p>

<h3>7. Plan the deprecations</h3>
<p>Roots, Sampling and Logging are deprecated, with suggested migrations: pass paths through tool parameters or configuration instead of Roots, call model providers directly instead of Sampling, and use stderr or OpenTelemetry instead of protocol Logging<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Trace context now has documented conventions in <code>_meta</code>, which fits the approach in <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Stateless MCP doesn't make your system stateless. It makes your state visible, which is exactly where an auditor, an SRE and the model all need it to be.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Sequencing for an enterprise estate</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Gateway first</strong>, so routing and policy work for both old and new protocol versions during the transition.</li>
<li><strong>High-traffic, stateless servers next</strong>: the easiest wins for scalability.</li>
<li><strong>Interactive and stateful servers last</strong>, with handles and MRTR designed and tested properly.</li>
<li><strong>Registry metadata updated</strong> to show which protocol versions each server supports, as in <a href="/insights/mcp-registry-enterprise-subregistry/">an enterprise MCP registry</a>.</li>
</ol>
</section>

